# StaticForge — web application configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` in this directory (apps/web/.env) and fill in the real values.
# Next.js loads it from the app root, not from the repository root — a copy at
# the top level is read by Prisma and ignored by the web tier, which is the
# usual reason "I set the variable and nothing changed".
#
# Placeholders are rejected at runtime rather than accepted and quietly failing
# later: a Supabase client pointed at "your-project" fails further from the
# mistake than this file does. The error names the variable and says it is
# still a placeholder, so a half-filled copy is diagnosed rather than mysterious.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required for anyone to sign in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Supabase project the dashboard verifies user sessions against.
# Found under Project Settings → API in the Supabase dashboard.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# The *anon* key, not the service-role key.
#
# This value verifies someone else's token; it does not need to bypass row
# security and must not be able to. A service-role key here would give the
# web tier unrestricted database access through an environment variable that
# is copied between machines.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Without both of the above the app still runs and still serves integrations
# holding API keys — a missing identity provider is one outage, not two. What
# it cannot do is let a person sign in: `/login` answers 500, and every request
# arriving without a header is answered 401 rather than being told the server
# is misconfigured.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Extra origins a cookie session may send mutations from. Comma separated;
# either bare hosts or full URLs.
#
# Cookie-authenticated writes must carry an `Origin` matching this server's own
# host. That is not something `SameSite=Lax` already does: SameSite compares
# *registrable domains*, so a POST from `evil.example.com` to `app.example.com`
# is same-site and carries the cookie. Anyone who can put content on a sibling
# subdomain can otherwise forge authenticated writes.
#
# Set this only when the dashboard is genuinely served from a different host
# than the API — a separate front end, or local development against a deployed
# API. Every entry is a host allowed to spend a signed-in person's session, so
# an entry added "temporarily" is a permanent one until somebody audits it.
#
# Bearer callers are unaffected and need no entry here: an attacker cannot make
# a browser attach a header it does not know.
# STATICFORGE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=dashboard.example.com,https://staging.example.com

# Who this deployment treats as the platform itself, as opposed to a tenant of
# it. Guards operations no customer role may perform — setting a plan's ceiling,
# for one, since the OWNER who holds every tenant capability is exactly the
# person that ceiling bills.
#
# In development this defaults to `local-operator`, the identity the CLI runs
# as. **In production there is no default**: leave it unset and there is no
# platform operator at all, and every platform operation refuses. That is a
# deployment that cannot set quotas rather than one where a well-known string
# can, which is the recoverable way to be wrong.
# STATICFORGE_PLATFORM_OPERATOR=ops-alice
